  "notes": "An ichidan verb using the kanji {魅|み} (enchant/charm) instead of the standard {見|み} of {見|み}せる (to show). This spelling emphasizes the captivating or spellbinding quality of what is being shown.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {観客|かんきゃく}を{魅|み}せる (captivate the audience)\n- {演技|えんぎ}で{魅|み}せる (enchant with a performance)\n- {魅|み}せるプレー (a dazzling play/performance)\n\nUSAGE:\nOften appears in entertainment, sports, and arts contexts. The passive form {魅|み}せられる (to be captivated) is also common. Related to {魅了|みりょう}する (to fascinate) and {魅力|みりょく} (charm/appeal), all sharing the kanji {魅|み}. In casual writing, sometimes used interchangeably with {見|み}せる to add dramatic flair, but the distinction is meaningful in careful writing.",
